GitLab Runner: smallest resource-only configuration
===================================================

The checked ConfigMap named gitlab-runner-config is generated by the
GitLab Runner Operator. Do not add the lines to that generated ConfigMap
because the Operator can overwrite them.

There are two small changes:

1. Create a new ConfigMap containing the resource lines.
2. Add its name to spec.config in the existing Runner resource.


Change 1: create the resource ConfigMap
=======================================

In OpenShift, create this YAML:

---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: gitlab-runner-job-resources
  namespace: gitlab-system
data:
  config.toml: |
    [[runners]]
      [runners.kubernetes]
        cpu_request = "1"
        cpu_limit = "4"
        memory_request = "2Gi"
        memory_limit = "6Gi"
---

Apply it:

oc apply -f gitlab-runner-job-resources.yaml


Change 2: update the existing Runner
====================================

Open:

OpenShift -> Installed Operators -> GitLab Runner -> gitlab-runner -> YAML

Find the existing spec section:

spec:
  buildImage: alpine
  ca: gitlab-cert
  gitlabUrl: 'https://gitlab.apps.asmonpeclr.np.asmo.com'
  token: gitlab-runner-secret

Add this one line under spec:

  config: gitlab-runner-job-resources

The final spec must look like this:

spec:
  buildImage: alpine
  ca: gitlab-cert
  config: gitlab-runner-job-resources
  gitlabUrl: 'https://gitlab.apps.asmonpeclr.np.asmo.com'
  token: gitlab-runner-secret

Click Save.

Alternatively, run:

oc patch runner gitlab-runner -n gitlab-system --type merge \
  -p '{"spec":{"config":"gitlab-runner-job-resources"}}'


Verify
======

oc get runner gitlab-runner -n gitlab-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.config}{"\n"}'

Expected:

gitlab-runner-job-resources

Run a new pipeline and inspect its job pod:

oc get pods -n gitlab-system

oc describe pod <JOB-POD-NAME> -n gitlab-system

The build container should show:

Requests:
  cpu: 1
  memory: 2Gi

Limits:
  cpu: 4
  memory: 6Gi


Important
=========

This is the smallest configuration for build pod CPU and memory only.
It does not change the Runner manager pod, concurrency, cache, storage,
job timeout, or GitLab artifact upload limit.


Nexus Container Registry
========================

Nexus repository:

Name: marketplace
Format: docker
Type: hosted
Routing: Path based routing

The Docker image name uses /marketplace/, not /repository/marketplace/.

The pipeline will push:

nexus.apps.asmonpeclr.np.asmo.com/marketplace/mc3s-b2x-asmo-productcatalog:$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA
nexus.apps.asmonpeclr.np.asmo.com/marketplace/mc3s-b2x-asmo-productcatalog:$CI_COMMIT_REF_SLUG

Go to:

GitLab project -> Settings -> CI/CD -> Variables

Confirm these two masked variables exist:

Key:
NEXUS_USERNAME

Value:
devops

Key:
NEXUS_PASSWORD

Value:
<NEXUS DEVOPS USER PASSWORD>

For both variables:

- Select Masked.
- Select Protected only if pipelines run only on protected branches.
- Do not add these values to .gitlab-ci.yml.

The Nexus devops user must have permission to browse, read, add, and edit
components in the marketplace Docker repository.

The image job uses Maven Jib on the OCP Runner. It does not require Docker,
Docker-in-Docker, Kaniko, a Dockerfile, or a privileged Runner.

It consumes the JAR artifact from build_maven and pushes two image tags:

$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA
$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:$CI_COMMIT_REF_SLUG

The pipeline reads the credentials from:

$NEXUS_USERNAME
$NEXUS_PASSWORD

Jib reads the Nexus credentials from the masked GitLab variables and pushes
the image directly. The credentials are not stored in the repository.

Because the internal Nexus route may use a private certificate, the current
Jib command includes:

-Djib.allowInsecureRegistries=true

Remove this option after the Nexus CA certificate is trusted by the
Runner.

Kaniko is not used because the restricted OpenShift user cannot unpack and
replace operating-system files such as /bin/sh. Jib builds image layers
without unpacking a root filesystem.

Before running the pipeline, test from the VDI:

curl -k -I https://nexus.apps.asmonpeclr.np.asmo.com/v2/

The response should be 200 or 401, not 404 or 503.

After the pipeline succeeds, open the Nexus marketplace repository and browse
the mc3s-b2x-asmo-productcatalog image.
